HTML Full Document Structure
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <meta name="description" content="Learning HTML">
    <title>My First Web Page</title>
</head>

<body>

    <h1>Welcome to HTML</h1>
    <p>This is my first webpage.</p>

</body>

</html>


Line-by-Line Explanation
1. <!DOCTYPE html>
<!DOCTYPE html>
Purpose
Declares that the document is written in HTML5.
It tells the browser how to interpret the document.

Interview Question
Q: Why do we use <!DOCTYPE html>?
Answer:
It tells the browser to render the page in HTML5 standards mode. Without it, browsers may switch to quirks mode,
 which can lead to inconsistent rendering.

2. <html>
<html lang="en">
Purpose
Root element of the webpage.
Everything else is inside it.
lang="en"
This specifies that the content is in English.
Benefits:
Better accessibility.
Improved search engine optimization (SEO).
Helps browsers and screen readers choose the correct pronunciation.

3. <head>
<head>
The <head> section contains metadata about the page. This information is used by browsers and search engines
     but is not displayed as visible page content.

4. <meta charset="UTF-8">
Purpose
Specifies the character encoding.
UTF-8 supports:
English
Telugu
Hindi
Chinese
Emojis 😊
Almost every language
Without UTF-8, some characters may appear incorrectly.

5. Viewport Meta Tag
<meta name="viewport"
content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
Purpose
Makes the webpage responsive.
Without it:
Mobile browsers may zoom out.
Text and layouts can appear too small.
With it:
The page scales correctly to the device width.

6. Description Meta Tag
<meta name="description"
content="Learning HTML">
Purpose
Provides a short description of the webpage.
Search engines often use this text in search results, which can improve discoverability.

7. <title>
<title>My Website</title>
Purpose
The title appears:
On the browser tab.
In bookmarks.
In search engine results.

8. <body>
<body>
Everything visible on the webpage goes inside the <body>.
Examples:
Headings
Paragraphs
Images
Tables
Forms
Videos
Buttons
Basic HTML Elements

Headings
<h1>Main Heading</h1>
<h2>Sub Heading</h2>
<h3>Section</h3>
<h4>Topic</h4>
<h5>Small Heading</h5>
<h6>Smallest Heading</h6>

There are six heading levels:
h1 is the most important.
h6 is the least important.
Interview Tip: A page should generally have one primary h1 describing the main content.

